title: Review — walk the list backwards
chapter: 32
type: scenario
skills: code-review, speed-under-pressure, quickfix-nav, make-errorformat
par: 110
minutes: 10
vscode: five comments in a browser tab, five hunts through two files
open: review.txt
vimrc: set hidden errorformat=%f:%l:\ %m

@brief
    !344  Five comments. Your reviewer exports them as `file:line:
          message`, because she got tired of people missing the
          fourth one.

That file is open. It is a list of positions in files, which is exactly
what the quickfix list is, so do not read it and go hunting — load it and
let it take you there.

    src/domain/order/order.provider.ts:14  'PENDING' is now 'AWAITING_PAYMENT'
    src/domain/order/order.provider.ts:18  leftover console.log, the line goes
    src/domain/order/order.provider.ts:25  magic number, APPROVAL_LIMIT exists
    src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts:2   exception is not used in this file
    src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts:17  dead method, it and the gap above it

Neither file is open. You do not need to open either of them by hand.

**The budget is 110 keystrokes.** It assumes you never type a file
name and never type a line number.

Do not change review.txt.

@keys
| `:cfile review.txt` | load a `file:line: message` list as the quickfix list |
| `:clast` `:cp` | the last entry, then the one before |
| `:cn` `:cc` | the next entry, the current one |
| `ciw` `ci'` | change the word, change inside the quotes |

@hints
Two of the five comments delete a line. Deleting a line moves everything
below it, and three of the entries are below. Think about what that does
to line numbers you have not visited yet.
--
It does nothing at all to them if you visit them first. Start at the end
of the list and walk up: every entry you have already dealt with is below
the one you are on, so nothing you have done can have moved it.
--
`:cfile review.txt`, `:clast`, then `:cp` four times, doing the edit at
each stop. `k4dd` for the dead method, `dd` for the import, `f5` `ciw`
for the magic number, `dd` for the log, `f'` `ci'` for the status.

@solution
`:cfile review.txt` `:clast` `k4dd` `:cp` `dd` `:cp` `f5ciwAPPROVAL_LIMIT`
`Esc` `:cp` `dd` `:cp` `f'ci'AWAITING_PAYMENT` `Esc` `:wqa`.
96 keystrokes, and you never typed a path.

**A review is a quickfix list.** Anything shaped like `file:line: message`
loads with `:cfile`, and from that moment the review is navigation rather
than searching. Most review tools will export this, and `git grep -n`,
`tsc`, `eslint --format compact` and `jest` all produce it natively. The
habit worth forming is noticing the shape: the moment you find yourself
reading a list of file names and line numbers off a screen and typing
them back in, there is a `:cfile`, a `:grep` or a `:make` that would have
done it.

**Backwards is not a trick, it is the general rule.** Any time you apply
a list of positional edits to a file, work from the bottom up. Line
numbers below your cursor move when you insert or delete; line numbers
above it never do. `:clast` and `:cp` cost the same as `:cfirst` and
`:cn`, and one of them is correct regardless of what your edits do to the
line count. Batch tools like `:cdo` sidestep this by re-resolving each
entry as they go — but the moment you are steering by hand, go backwards.

**The errorformat.** Vim's default recognises `file:line:message`, and
this lesson sets it explicitly anyway so the list parses the same on any
machine. If `:cfile` gives you a list of entries with no file attached,
that is almost always the errorformat and almost never the file: check it
with `:set errorformat?` before you start editing the list by hand.

The comment you should look hardest at is the third one. "Magic number,
`APPROVAL_LIMIT` exists" is only correct if the constant means the same
thing here as it does where it was defined — five lines up in this case,
so it does. When the constant lives in another module and a reviewer
suggests reusing it, go and read it first. Two things being 50000 today
is not the same as two things being the same number.

@start review.txt
src/domain/order/order.provider.ts:14: 'PENDING' was renamed to 'AWAITING_PAYMENT' in !301
src/domain/order/order.provider.ts:18: leftover console.log, the whole line goes
src/domain/order/order.provider.ts:25: magic number, APPROVAL_LIMIT is five lines up
src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts:2: exception is not used in this file
src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts:17: dead since !298, the method and the blank line above it

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

const APPROVAL_LIMIT = 50000;

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async place(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'PENDING' } });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    console.log('cancelling', id);
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.total > 50000) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NEEDS_APPROVAL');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

const APPROVAL_LIMIT = 50000;

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async place(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'AWAITING_PAYMENT' } });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.total > APPROVAL_LIMIT) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NEEDS_APPROVAL');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async placeOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.place(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }

  public async legacyCancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async placeOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.place(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'AWAITING_PAYMENT' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';
